'''''Sophora macrocarpa''''' is a species of flowering tree or shrub of the genus ''[[Sophora]]'' of the family [[Fabaceae]]. It is commonly known as '''mayú''' or '''mayo''', and is [[Endemism|endemic]] to central and southern continental Chile.<ref>{{cite book|title=Bosque|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Mkw9AQAAIAAJ |year=1990 |publisher=Universidad Austral de Chile |language=Spanish |pages=73–4}}</ref>

==Description==
''Sophora macrocarpa'' is a small evergreen tree or large shrub, growing to a height of about  {{convert|3|m|0|abbr=on}} with long slender branches . The new growth is pubescent, with reddish-brown hairs. The leaves are alternate and up to {{convert|15|cm|0|abbr=on}} in length. They are pinnate, each with nine to fifteen leaflets. The leaflets are dark green on the upperside and slightly glaucous underneath. The dangling clusters of flowers have long thick stems. Each flower has five pale brown calyx lobes fused into a cup, five long yellow petals and ten stamens. The fruit is a pod containing several seeds. The flowering period is August to December.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://museo.florachilena.cl/Niv_tax/Angiospermas/Ordenes/Fabales/Fabaceae/Sophora/mayu/Mayu.htm |title=''Sophora macrocarpa'' |language=Spanish |work=Enciclopedia de la Flora Chilena |accessdate=2015-05-26}}</ref>

